WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.701
-Our first guest tonight
is a talented musician

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:04.067
and actress
you know from her work

00:00:04.067 --> 00:00:06.167
on shows like "Younger"
and "Lizzie McGuire."

00:00:06.167 --> 00:00:09.133
She both stars in and produces
"How I Met Your Father."

00:00:09.133 --> 00:00:11.300
The second season begins
streaming on Hulu tomorrow,

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.267
with new episodes available
on Tuesdays.

00:00:13.267 --> 00:00:14.634
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.634 --> 00:00:16.667
-Why don't you
go grab us some drinks

00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:18.100
and snag us a table?
-Oh, sure.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.901
Uh, what do you want to drink?
-Just surprise me.

00:00:19.901 --> 00:00:23.400
Listen, everyone,
I don't want Ian to know

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.167
that I've burned through
two different guys

00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:26.400
in the past day.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.200
So everyone be cool and chill,
just like me.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.334
Cool and chill, yeah?
Let me hear you say it.

00:00:31.334 --> 00:00:33.501
What am I?
-Cool and chill.

00:00:33.501 --> 00:00:36.801
-Alright. Now let's do me.
I am tall and curious.

00:00:36.801 --> 00:00:40.367
-Please welcome back to
the show Hilary Duff, everybody!

00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:42.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:42.601 --> 00:00:45.601
[ Mid-tempo music plays ]

00:00:45.601 --> 00:00:50.734
♪♪

00:00:50.734 --> 00:00:55.834
♪♪

00:00:55.834 --> 00:00:58.701
Welcome back!
I'm so happy to have you.

00:00:58.701 --> 00:01:00.267
-Thank you so much.
-Hi, Hilary!

00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:02.067
-Ah!
-There you go. Look at that.

00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:03.200
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Ah.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.501
-Last time,
you were here on Zoom.

00:01:04.501 --> 00:01:06.167
The audience couldn't react
with the same enthusiasm.

00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:07.801
-You didn't give me that
on Zoom.

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:09.234
-No, I didn't.
-No.

00:01:09.234 --> 00:01:11.968
-I think I was like, "Couldn't
make the trip, huh, Hilary?"

00:01:11.968 --> 00:01:14.567
[ Laughter ]
I do want to check in with you

00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:16.000
because you were saying,
last time we spoke,

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.968
that you had taught yourself
to play guitar a little bit.

00:01:18.968 --> 00:01:20.734
You taught yourself
a couple songs

00:01:20.734 --> 00:01:22.267
people always really ask for
at parties --

00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:24.133
the "Batman" theme and
the "Mission: Impossible" theme.

00:01:24.133 --> 00:01:26.167
-I'm really popular at parties.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.167 --> 00:01:28.634
-She goes, "Who wants to hear
the 'Batman' theme?"

00:01:28.634 --> 00:01:31.100
-Yeah, you know
what's really, really --

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.968
I knew were gonna ask me this,
and if you

00:01:33.968 --> 00:01:36.133
put a guitar in my lap,
I couldn't do it anymore.

00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:38.234
I decided to have another child.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:01:38.234 --> 00:01:41.801
-Crushed all my personal dreams.
-So you actually had two kids.

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:43.701
You were like, "Am I gonna be
a professional guitarist

00:01:43.701 --> 00:01:46.100
or have a third child?"
-Yeah. We chose --

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.367
-That's lovely
that you chose family.

00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:50.167
That's really sweet.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:53.167
We also -- I wanted to check in
because your middle --

00:01:53.167 --> 00:01:55.501
your daughter, wild child.
-Yeah.

00:01:55.501 --> 00:01:57.400
-Is she still?
-You know what?

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.601
She's -- I don't want to say
she's a wild child,

00:02:00.601 --> 00:02:02.701
because she's not bad.
She's just, like,

00:02:02.701 --> 00:02:04.734
the things
that come out of her mouth

00:02:04.734 --> 00:02:06.367
and the way
that her brain thinks,

00:02:06.367 --> 00:02:08.968
she's like a unicorn.
She's just --

00:02:08.968 --> 00:02:11.000
-Which is wonderful.
-She surprises us all the time.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.100
-Our middle one is the same way.
-Is that a middle-child thing?

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.400
-Maybe it's a middle child.
Our youngest --

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:17.734
and I think your youngest
is the same way --

00:02:17.734 --> 00:02:19.200
it's a little too early
to tell exactly.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.400
-Yeah.
-But we feel like

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.868
our youngest is wired
the way our oldest is,

00:02:22.868 --> 00:02:24.667
and the one in the middle is
just on his own frequency.

00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:26.534
-I feel the same way.
I feel the same way.

00:02:26.534 --> 00:02:31.467
The first and the third are
just, like, a little calmer.

00:02:31.467 --> 00:02:34.501
Like, they can just -- just be,

00:02:34.501 --> 00:02:38.200
and Banks is, like, on the couch
like, existing.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.033
And actually, we're all just
existing around her orbit.

00:02:41.033 --> 00:02:43.300
You know?
-My middle one is doing

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.968
a thing recently
where he'll scream "Daddy,"

00:02:45.968 --> 00:02:48.734
and I'll run in, and then --
-That never happens

00:02:48.734 --> 00:02:51.067
in my household.
-Oh, really?

00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:53.367
Nobody -- They just have, like,
given up on the dad showing up?

00:02:53.367 --> 00:02:55.567
-Well, it's not -- He's there.
-He's there,

00:02:55.567 --> 00:02:57.067
but he's not a problem solver?
-And he's like, "I'm

00:02:57.067 --> 00:02:59.267
perfectly willing and able."
It's just they all want me.

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:00.767
-Oh, really?
-Yeah.

00:03:00.767 --> 00:03:02.667
-Well, look at me.
-I mean, look at me.

00:03:02.667 --> 00:03:04.634
[ Laughter ]

00:03:04.634 --> 00:03:05.834
-He'll say "Daddy,"
and then I'll run in,

00:03:05.834 --> 00:03:07.968
and he'll be playing
with, like, toy men.

00:03:07.968 --> 00:03:10.801
And he'll be like,
"No, not you. His daddy."

00:03:10.801 --> 00:03:13.234
So he's, like, play-acting
with toy daddies.

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:15.834
And then there's
only so many times

00:03:15.834 --> 00:03:18.200
you can hear him scream "Daddy,"
and, like, you're sitting there,

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.367
and you're like,
"It's not me. It's the toy."

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:22.167
-Meanwhile, he's, like, bleeding
in the room next door.

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:23.934
-I went in one time, and
he had a massive head injury.

00:03:23.934 --> 00:03:25.300
No.
-Oh, my God.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.400
[ Laughter ]
Oh, my God.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.200
-You did -- This is --

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.267
Again, having three kids,
I know the feeling.

00:03:31.267 --> 00:03:33.534
And your holiday card,
you really leaned in

00:03:33.534 --> 00:03:35.601
to how chaotic
the three kids can be.

00:03:35.601 --> 00:03:39.667
Here, you guys are basically
wrapped up like hostages.

00:03:39.667 --> 00:03:42.667
-Yeah.
-With Christmas tree lights.

00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:44.567
Now, do the kids enjoy --
I mean, obviously

00:03:44.567 --> 00:03:46.434
they understand what
the staging here is,

00:03:46.434 --> 00:03:48.701
is that they are basically --
you're captives to their whims.

00:03:48.701 --> 00:03:51.267
-I mean isn't that
the generic theme

00:03:51.267 --> 00:03:53.033
running through
our whole entire life?

00:03:53.033 --> 00:03:54.434
-I guess so,
but do the kids know?

00:03:54.434 --> 00:03:55.968
Like, when you're like, "Hey,
we're gonna take a picture

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:57.133
where you guys
look like maniacs,

00:03:57.133 --> 00:03:59.968
and we're, like, living
as your prisoners,

00:03:59.968 --> 00:04:01.667
and they're like,
"Yeah, that sounds right."

00:04:01.667 --> 00:04:04.267
-Yeah, Luca was aware,
and he was really into

00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:07.467
tying us up
in the Christmas lights.

00:04:07.467 --> 00:04:09.334
Banks was just thrilled
to have a cupcake.

00:04:09.334 --> 00:04:10.934
And same with Mae.
Sorry.

00:04:10.934 --> 00:04:14.167
Mae was happy to have a cupcake,
and Banks was like,

00:04:14.167 --> 00:04:16.067
"Do I get a treat after this?
Great."

00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:18.000
-I will say, that is very smart
to actually have

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.934
eatable cupcakes on-set.
-Yeah.

00:04:20.934 --> 00:04:22.701
I mean, the rug went to [Bleep]
afterwards.

00:04:22.701 --> 00:04:24.834
-Yeah.
You didn't think the --

00:04:24.834 --> 00:04:27.868
I really enjoy
"All is not calm".

00:04:27.868 --> 00:04:29.200
-Yeah.
Did you see the top of --

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:31.634
I was really proud of the top.
We have a blended family,

00:04:31.634 --> 00:04:34.000
so you've got to kind of
just make light of it.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.100
-Uh-huh.
-Duffs and Bairs and a Comrie.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.934
There's lots of last names
in our household, so...

00:04:38.934 --> 00:04:40.367
[ Laughter ]

00:04:40.367 --> 00:04:43.367
-You -- This is Banks
outed you.

00:04:43.367 --> 00:04:45.734
-Oh, Seth, are you gonna
do this to me?

00:04:45.734 --> 00:04:48.467
-Well, you put it --
You can't put it on social media

00:04:48.467 --> 00:04:52.067
and then be like, "Oh, that was
a secret I told everyone."

00:04:52.067 --> 00:04:53.300
[ Laughter ]

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:55.501
-I do that sometimes, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.501 --> 00:04:57.634
I'm actually excited
to say "diarrhea" on your show

00:04:57.634 --> 00:04:59.334
as many times as possible.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:04:59.334 --> 00:05:00.934
-I'm gonna start right now.
-That was the first one.

00:05:00.934 --> 00:05:03.000
-You share the story.
-You did say

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.467
Banks told a story at camp.
"My mom made bad chicken,

00:05:05.467 --> 00:05:07.467
and now she has..."
-Diarrhea.

00:05:07.467 --> 00:05:08.701
[ Laughter ]

00:05:08.701 --> 00:05:10.334
-That's what happens
when you make bad chicken.

00:05:10.334 --> 00:05:13.033
What I like is, what happened
at soccer camp

00:05:13.033 --> 00:05:14.934
that she thought, "I actually
have something

00:05:14.934 --> 00:05:17.033
that will add
to the conversation"?

00:05:17.033 --> 00:05:19.601
-I don't think
she was prompted at all.

00:05:19.601 --> 00:05:23.300
I think she just walked up --
By the way, her soccer coach,

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.334
very good-looking man.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:05:25.334 --> 00:05:27.534
-Very good-looking man.
And I was at work.

00:05:27.534 --> 00:05:31.234
Thank God I was at work.
But my nanny called

00:05:31.234 --> 00:05:34.934
and said, "Well, now,
the whole entire soccer class

00:05:34.934 --> 00:05:37.601
and coach knows
that you ate bad chicken.

00:05:37.601 --> 00:05:39.167
You made the bad chicken,
and now you're

00:05:39.167 --> 00:05:40.767
paying for the bad chicken."
-Yeah.

00:05:40.767 --> 00:05:42.701
[ Laughter ]
-Like, not cool, Banks.

00:05:42.701 --> 00:05:45.267
And she was like,
"My mom has diarrhea."

00:05:45.267 --> 00:05:46.534
[ Laughter ]

00:05:46.534 --> 00:05:48.300
-To be honest, it's
really a double burn,

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:50.133
because, one, you have diarrhea.
You don't want people --

00:05:50.133 --> 00:05:51.434
-I wasn't having a great day.
-But also it's like,

00:05:51.434 --> 00:05:53.934
"My mom has diarrhea,
and it's her fault."

00:05:53.934 --> 00:05:56.300
-Yeah, yeah, and,
"She undercooked the chicken

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:58.501
and almost poisoned us all."
No one ate it but me.

00:05:58.501 --> 00:06:01.267
[ Laughter ]
-Congratulations on the show.

00:06:01.267 --> 00:06:04.100
A really cool thing happens --
happened, I should say --

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:05.634
which I feel like
doesn't happen in TV anymore.

00:06:05.634 --> 00:06:07.334
You did a 10-episode
first season,

00:06:07.334 --> 00:06:09.667
and it went so well and
people liked it so much,

00:06:09.667 --> 00:06:11.234
you're doing
a 20-episode season now.

00:06:11.234 --> 00:06:14.300
That's very old-school,
the 20-episode sitcom season.

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:17.033
Was it really rewarding
when you all got the news,

00:06:17.033 --> 00:06:18.701
"Hey, we're gonna do
twice as much"?

00:06:18.701 --> 00:06:21.701
-Oh, gosh. I mean,
you know how this business is.

00:06:21.701 --> 00:06:22.968
You get a phone call,
and you're just like...

00:06:22.968 --> 00:06:24.567
-Yeah.
-"Are we getting picked up?

00:06:24.567 --> 00:06:26.467
Are we getting fired?
Are we getting hired again?"

00:06:26.467 --> 00:06:28.033
I don't know what the --
you know, you're kind of always

00:06:28.033 --> 00:06:30.701
on the edge of your seat.
And to get that news,

00:06:30.701 --> 00:06:32.334
it was really exciting.
You know?

00:06:32.334 --> 00:06:34.033
We knew the numbers
were really good

00:06:34.033 --> 00:06:37.467
from the first season,
but to double-order the show

00:06:37.467 --> 00:06:39.601
and just give us
that extra creative space

00:06:39.601 --> 00:06:42.767
for the story lines
to just, you know, dive deeper

00:06:42.767 --> 00:06:44.200
into each character
and get to have

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:47.200
a little bit more fun and
bring on, you know, more people,

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:50.100
it was -- it was really, like,
just --

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.467
you just sign on to something,
hoping it works.

00:06:52.467 --> 00:06:55.167
And it was working.
-It's really nice.

00:06:55.167 --> 00:06:56.567
It's really exciting
to be on the upswing.

00:06:56.567 --> 00:07:00.767
You mentioned adding new people.
Uh, John Corbett.

00:07:00.767 --> 00:07:02.267
-John Corbett, yeah.
There he is.

00:07:02.267 --> 00:07:04.767
-John Corbett,
love interest now.

00:07:04.767 --> 00:07:06.868
Also played your music teacher
a long time ago.

00:07:06.868 --> 00:07:10.434
-He sure did.
And now we are smooching it up.

00:07:10.434 --> 00:07:12.834
-This would have been very bad
if that happened --

00:07:12.834 --> 00:07:15.400
-Very, very bad.
It did not happen then.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:18.200
-A lot of time passed,
and then it's an age difference

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.200
that everybody
has got to be cool with.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:22.133
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
I mean, she's 35 now.

00:07:22.133 --> 00:07:23.734
-Yeah, it's totally fine.
-Cool.

00:07:23.734 --> 00:07:27.334
-Are you
a "Sex and the City" fan?

00:07:27.334 --> 00:07:29.334
-I mean, yes. Aren't we all?

00:07:29.334 --> 00:07:31.367
-Yeah. I should say --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:31.367 --> 00:07:33.033
I'll be honest -- late convert.

00:07:33.033 --> 00:07:34.868
I sort of missed it
when it happened.

00:07:34.868 --> 00:07:36.868
You know, my wife, Alexi,
made me watch it all,

00:07:36.868 --> 00:07:38.734
and totally bought it.
Loved it.

00:07:38.734 --> 00:07:40.434
-I mean, happy wife,
happy life, right?

00:07:40.434 --> 00:07:42.133
-Yeah, exactly.
But I should note,

00:07:42.133 --> 00:07:43.634
I was also happy
when I watched it.

00:07:43.634 --> 00:07:46.601
I wasn't just like --
-No, it's a forever goody.

00:07:46.601 --> 00:07:49.334
But also, like, in my mind,
he's Aidan, so...

00:07:49.334 --> 00:07:52.467
-Yeah.
-I did call him John on-set,

00:07:52.467 --> 00:07:54.234
but I have to suffer --
not suffer through.

00:07:54.234 --> 00:07:57.067
But, you know, a lot of my life,
like, I'm called Lizzie.

00:07:57.067 --> 00:07:59.200
So he's Aidan.
That's what it is.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:00.667
-If he complained
about it,

00:08:00.667 --> 00:08:02.133
you're like,
"Buddy, get in line."

00:08:02.133 --> 00:08:03.601
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:03.601 --> 00:08:05.067
-What a delight
to have you back.

00:08:05.067 --> 00:08:07.367
Thank you so much for being
here, and do come back anytime.

00:08:07.367 --> 00:08:09.834
You guys, give it up
for Hilary Duff.

00:08:09.834 --> 00:08:13.133
The second season
of "How I Met Your Father"

00:08:13.133 --> 00:08:14.434
begins streaming tomorrow
on Hulu.

00:08:14.434 --> 00:08:15.634
We'll be right back
with Kim Petras.

